this next story takes us to jakarta  where a group of young professionals are  embodying what it means to be part of  the so-called solidarity economy  [Music]  this mega city has been hard hit by the  pandemic jakarta capital of indonesia  and southeast asia's fastest growing  internet economy  in its darkest hour one man a hobby  musician turned this to his advantage  lodi andrean teamed up with some friends  to stand strong in the face of the  pandemic  we didn't know how long this will last  it wasn't clear what disease this was  and we didn't know when it will end so  from there actually from that  uncertainty we decided to do something  and this is what he came up with  scrolling her instagram account marita  putri who had just lost her job in an  advertising agency discovered bagirata  an initiative that promised financial  aid without red tape  actually i had no income other than  selling breakfast  my husband used to work but he was laid  off too in the pandemic  so to feed the family i tried to sign up  for this platform baguirata  to have at least some funds to keep my  breakfast business going  model work  bhagirata is a wealth distribution  platform where the name reflects the aim  it translates to equal share because  this is what lodi andrean felt was  needed most in the lockdown  with four friends the 29 year old  designer founded the non-profit  organization  bugirata is a cross-subsidy platform for  the people who are affected by the  pandemic  especially in formal workers who don't  get government assistance or who don't  get help from their offices and  freelancers  so the platform itself allows people who  can help financially to get in contact  with people who need help  likewise the latter can say what they  need  more than 4 000 people from all over  indonesia have already received money  through bhagirata  the founders have distributed around 90  000 euros in donations the key to  success was that andrean took advantage  of an existing digital infrastructure  that had been built up in indonesia in  recent years he programmed bhagirata as  a direct transfer system where the money  doesn't have to go through their hands  donors can directly send funds to  beneficiaries through their e-wallet  almost everybody here uses apps like ovo  gopay or dana and mobile phones are  affordable the advertisement also takes  place online  what turned out to be more time  consuming however was the verification  process the team wanted to make sure  that people didn't register twice  moreover they wanted to only help people  hit by the pandemic  so what we do is actually a full  feasibility assessment  we check if the profession of the person  applying is really affected  is he or she a business owner or an  employee  and of course was the business disrupted  by the pandemic or are there other  reasons like a drop in demand it's a  time consuming procedure for five people  particularly after hours but for donors  like hariya rivani this is extremely  important the creative director  regularly uses the platform to help  people in need  it's definitely better  meaning that the concept of bhagiratha  is clearly better than what the  government has to offer  for one thing i can list the people i  want to support and i also have more  confidence in the spirit of the people  in bhagirata  the spirit of solidarity is great and  there is no other interest because they  really want to help each other and in my  opinion that's a significant thing  indeed  rivani feels privileged to be able to  work from home if another wave builds up  like right now with omicron  so he is happy to share some of his  money he also appreciates that via the  app he can remain anonymous while still  knowing who will receive his money  actually we're not all in the same boat  because there's so many people who  really need help  so it's as simple as that i look at  people's profile i see for example what  kind of reason he or she has to apply  then i check their background a bit  those kind of things well i'm not that  picky  initially bugirata was planned as a  short-term project three months at most  that was two years ago  now we still stick with it when the  pandemic is over and mobility returns to  normal and the economy starts to improve  we will close this platform however the  pandemic is still ongoing therefore  bhagiratha will probably continue to  help hundreds of people  marretta putri no longer needs the  platform at least for now her husband  found a new job and the 28 year old  mother of two is now optimistic that the  country's economy will recover too  she puts her trust in the official  booster campaign for which she'll  register with what else  her smartphone  we're joined by dw's asia pacific bureau  chief georg matas he's also the author  of that report  georg you follow the people we saw in  the story there for months what's the  situation like now for indonesians  effectively the situation right now is  that the economy here has practically  bounced back from the  from the time in summer last year when  indonesia was hard hit by the delta wave  now it's a bit of an ironical situation  here that we still see parts of the  economy opening up and parts of the  economy preparing for a potential  omicron wave to come the key difference  this time however is that the  vaccination campaign has been uh quite  successful 85 percent of people in  indonesia some 200 million people  eligible for vaccinations  have received their first dose 58 have  received the second dose and one percent  the booster and that will be key uh that  is part of the strategy of the  government where basically the  government puts its hope in  that this time they do not have to lock  down the economy as much and  place their hope into the effectiveness  of of this booster  now that's what the government is doing  but this story is an example of the  solidarity economy at work essentially  of people helping each other out does  that mean they don't think the  government is doing enough  i would say it's an example of of the  spirit that you have in indonesia that  people really reach out to help each  other to be fair when it comes to the  government one has to say that it's  facing huge challenges if you lock down  an economy like indonesia that creates  enormous problems and so the government  has put out some stimulus packages uh  some packages to deal with the health  sector in fact for this year alone uh 30  million 30 billion euros have been  earmarked for various projects uh a lot  of that will go into the health sector  but there is some controversy now  whether some of that money will is also  uh planned to be used for the  moving of the capital of jakarta to a  different island and that is what some  people reject because they say that the  problems we we have right now are  already so big  now you cover the asia pacific area for  us and a china slowdown is expected to  impact the wider region how big of a  concern is this where you are  look with indonesia you have the biggest  coal exporter with china you have the  biggest coal importer worldwide so they  do make a happy couple if things go well  if things don't go well you immediately  feel the  results here in indonesia in 2020 you  already had problems in the electric  industry uh when covet was raging in  china in 2021 the country was hard hit  because uh there was a huge drop in uh  in exports uh to uh china  china being the biggest uh exporting  destination for indonesia  and so uh pretty much everyone is  expecting that this will have an impact  on the relationship and now as well  particularly at a time when uh for  instance with nickel there's a lot of  corporations going on to be prepared for  the battery industry with cars  on the other hand a number of  indonesians have already set out that  they place also their hopes into the u.s  market because the u.s is keen to  increase its relations  with china with rather with indonesia in  order to decrease the influence of china  in this region  georg matas he's our asia pacific bureau  chief thank you very much for your time  today
 

